{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":1582736,"ns":0,"title":"Caliciviridae","revisions":[{"revid":1377341430,"parentid":1358064175,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-09-29T00:49:52Z","size":8358,"sha1":"71d3a6d8db386fa411d2ba428c5565f4b6e33d75","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Family of viruses}}\n{{Use dmy dates|date=June 2020}}\n{{Virusbox\n| image             = Calicivirus.jpg\n| image_alt         =\n| image_caption     =\n| taxon             = Caliciviridae\n| subdivision_ranks = Genera\n| subdivision       = See text\n| synonyms          =\n| synonyms_ref      =\n}}\n\nThe '''''Caliciviridae''''' are a family of \"small round structured\" [[viruses]], members of Class IV of the [[Baltimore scheme]]. Caliciviridae bear resemblance to enlarged picornavirus and were formerly considered to be a genus within the Picornaviridae.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Caliciviridae - Caliciviridae - Positive-sense RNA Viruses - ICTV|url=https://ictv.global/report/chapter/caliciviridae/caliciviridae|access-date=2021-03-12|website=talk.ictvonline.org|language=en}}</ref> They have [[positive-sense]], single-stranded [[RNA]] which is not segmented.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vinjé |first1=J |last2=Estes |first2=MK |last3=Esteves |first3=P |last4=Green |first4=KY |last5=Katayama |first5=K |last6=Knowles |first6=NJ |last7=L'Homme |first7=Y |last8=Martella |first8=V |last9=Vennema |first9=H |last10=White |first10=PA |last11=ICTV Report Consortium |title=ICTV Virus Taxonomy Profile: ''Caliciviridae''. |journal=The Journal of General Virology |date=November 2019 |volume=100 |issue=11 |pages=1469–1470 |doi=10.1099/jgv.0.001332 |pmid=31573467|pmc=7011698 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Thirteen species are placed in this family, divided among eleven genera.<ref name=\"ICTV\">{{cite web |title=ICTV Report Caliciviridae |url=http://www.ictv.global/report/caliciviridae}}</ref> Diseases associated with the '''calicivirus''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|k|æ|l|ɪ|s|ɪ|ˌ|v|aɪ|r|ə|s|,_|k|ə|ˈ|l|ɪ|s|ɪ|ˌ|v|aɪ|r|ə|s}}) family include [[feline_calicivirus| feline calicivirus]], a respiratory disease, rabbit hemorrhagic disease virus, which causes a usually fatal hepatitis, and the Norwalk group of viruses, which cause gastroenteritis.<ref name=ICTV/><ref name=ViralZone>{{cite web|title=Viral Zone|url=http://viralzone.expasy.org/all_by_species/32.html|publisher=ExPASy|access-date=15 June 2015}}</ref> Caliciviruses naturally infect [[vertebrate]]s, and have been found in a number of organisms such as [[humans]], [[cattle]], [[pig]]s, [[Feline calicivirus|cats]], [[chicken]]s, [[reptile]]s, [[dolphin]]s and [[amphibian]]s. The caliciviruses have a simple construction and are not [[viral envelope|enveloped]]. The [[capsid]] appears [[hexagonal]]/[[spherical]] and has [[icosahedral]] symmetry (T=1<ref>[http://viralzone.expasy.org/all_by_protein/1057.html T=1]</ref> or T=3<ref>[http://viralzone.expasy.org/all_by_protein/806.html T=3]</ref><ref name=ViralZone />) with a diameter of 35–39&nbsp;nm.<ref name= HansmanGS>{{cite book |editor= Hansman, GS| year=2010 |title=Caliciviruses: Molecular and Cellular Virology| publisher=[[Caister Academic Press]] | isbn= 978-1-904455-63-9}}</ref>\n\nCaliciviruses are not very well studied because until 2010, they could not be grown in [[cell culture|culture]], and they have a very narrow host range and no suitable animal model. However, the  application of modern genomic technologies has led to an increased understanding of the virus family.<ref name=\"HansmanGS\"/> A 2013 isolate from [[rhesus monkey]]s—[[Tulane virus]]—can be grown in culture, and this system promises to increase understanding of these viruses.<ref name=Yu2013>Yu G, Zhang D, Guo F, Tan M, Jiang X, Jiang W (2013). \"Cryo-EM structure of a novel calicivirus, Tulane Virus\". ''PLoS One'' 8(3):e59817. {{doi|10.1371/journal.pone.0059817|doi-access=free}}</ref>\n\n== Etymology ==\n''Calici''- comes from the Latin word ''calix'', which means \"cup\" or \"goblet\". This refers to the cup-shaped depressions on the surface of the virions of viruses in the family. The suffix -''viridae'' is the suffix used for virus families.<ref>{{cite web |title=Taxon Details: ''Caliciviridae'' |url=https://ictv.global/taxonomy/taxondetails?taxnode_id=202402798&taxon_name=Caliciviridae |publisher=International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses |access-date=1 August 2025}}</ref>\n\n==Taxonomy==\n[[File:Fmicb-10-01280-g001.svg|thumb|Phylogenetic tree of the family ''Caliciviridae'' and [[Poliovirus]]]]\n\nThe following genera are recognized:<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ictv.global/taxonomy|title=Virus Taxonomy: 2019 Release|website=talk.ictvonline.org|publisher=International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses|access-date=30 April 2020}}</ref>\n* ''[[Bavovirus]]''\n* ''[[Lagovirus]]''\n* ''[[Minovirus]]''\n* ''[[Nacovirus]]''\n* ''[[Nebovirus]]''\n* ''[[Norovirus]]''\n* ''[[Tulane virus|Recovirus]]''\n* ''[[Salovirus]]''\n* ''[[Sapovirus]]''\n* ''[[Valovirus]]''\n* ''[[Vesivirus]]''\n\nA number of other caliciviruses remain unclassified, including the chicken calicivirus.\n\n==Life cycle==\n[[File:Fmicb-10-01280-g002.jpg|thumb|Calicivirus genome comparison]]\nViral replication is cytoplasmic. Entry into the host cell is achieved by attachment to host receptors, which mediate endocytosis. Replication follows the positive-stranded RNA virus replication model. Positive-stranded RNA virus transcription is the method of transcription. Translation takes place by leaky scanning, and RNA termination-reinitiation. Vertebrates serve as the natural host. Transmission routes are fecal-oral.<ref name=ViralZone />\n\n== History ==\nEstablishing the viral etiology took many decades due to the difficulty of growing the virus in cell culture. In the 1940s and 1950s in the United States and Japan, caliciviridae could not be grown in culture, but as an experiment bacterial free filtrate of diarrhea was given to volunteers to check if viruses were present in volunteers' stool.<ref name=\"Themes\">{{Cite web|last=Themes|first=U. F. O.|date=2016-08-11|title=Caliciviridae: The Noroviruses|url=https://basicmedicalkey.com/caliciviridae-the-noroviruses/|access-date=2021-03-12|website=Basicmedical Key|language=en-US}}</ref> These experiments demonstrated that nonbacterial, filterable agents had the capability of causing [[enteric disease]] in humans.<ref name=\"Themes\"/> In 1968, an outbreak at a Norwalk elementary school (e.g. [[Norwalk Virus|Norwalk virus]]) in Ohio led to stool samples again being given to volunteers and serially passaged to other people. Finally, in 1972, Kapikian and his colleagues isolated the Norwalk virus from volunteers using [[immune electron microscopy]], a process that involves looking directly at antibody-antigen complexes.<ref name=\"Themes\"/> The classification of this one Norwalk virus strain served as the prototype for other species and small round structured viruses later known as [[Norovirus]].<ref name=\"Themes\"/>\n\n==Animal viruses==\n[[Rabbit hemorrhagic disease virus]] is a pathogen of rabbits that causes major problems throughout the world where rabbits are reared for food and clothing, make a significant contribution to [[ecosystem ecology]], and where they support valued wildlife as a food source.<ref name=\"HansmanGS\"/>\n\n== Uses ==\n[[Australia]] and [[New Zealand]], in an effort to control their rabbit populations, have intentionally spread rabbit calicivirus.\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n==External links==\n* [http://www.ictv.global/report/caliciviridae ICTV Report: ''Caliciviridae'']\n* [https://web.archive.org/web/20110723194343/http://www.ictvdb.org/ICTVdB/00.012.htm Caliciviridae] description page from the [[International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses]] site\n* [https://web.archive.org/web/20070213080825/http://www.microbiologybytes.com/virology/Caliciviruses.html MicrobiologyBytes: Caliciviruses]\n* [https://web.archive.org/web/20060109105556/http://www.ce.berkeley.edu/~nelson/ce210a/Calicivirus/calici.htm Human caliciviruses]\n* [http://www.stanford.edu/group/virus/calici/calici.html Stanford University]\n* [https://web.archive.org/web/20190913003110/https://www.viprbrc.org/brc/home.spg?decorator=calici Virus Pathogen Database and Analysis Resource (ViPR): Caliciviridae]\n* [http://www.expasy.org/viralzone/all_by_species/32.html Viralzone: Caliciviridae]\n* [http://www.pdbe.org/emsearch/calici*%20AND%20comp_type:virus 3D macromolecular structures of Caliciviridae from the EM Data Bank(EMDB)]\n* [http://ictvonline.org/virusTaxonomy.asp ICTV]\n\n{{Baltimore classification}}\n{{Taxonbar|from=Q1026758}}\n\n[[Category:Caliciviridae| ]]\n[[Category:Riboviria]]\n[[Category:Virus families]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-30T03:57:39Z","lastrevid":1377341430,"length":8358}]}}